Presidential elections were held in [[Colombia]] on 29 May 1994, with a second round on 19 June.<ref>{{cite book |first=Dieter |last=Nohlen |authorlink=Dieter Nohlen |date=2005 |title=Elections in the Americas: A data handbook, Volume II |page=306 |isbn=978-0-19928-358-3}}</ref> The result was a victory for [[Ernesto Samper]] of the [[Colombian Liberal Party]], who received 51% of the vote in the runoff.

Samper's victory was tainted by the [[Proceso 8000]] scandal, involving accusations that the Liberal Party had sought funding from the [[Cali Cartel]] during his campaign and afterward. This resulted in an investigation, which found several of Samper's close associates within the party guilty, although Samper himself was absolved of any wrongdoing.<ref>{{cite news |title=El proceso 8.000, a 21 años del escándalo mayor |url=http://www.elespectador.com/especiales/el-proceso-8000-20-anos-del-escandalo-mayor-articulo-556513 |newspaper=[[El Espectador]] |date=23 April 2015 |accessdate=8 November 2016}}</ref> However, the scandal badly damaged his and his party's reputation during his presidency and resulted in a coalition of opposition politicians forming to oppose him. This resulted in the defeat of the Liberal Party in the following [[1998 Colombian presidential election|1998 presidential election]]. As of the [[2022 Colombian presidential election|2022 presidential election]], Samper was Colombia's last Liberal Party president.

